{
	title: 'Compare and explicitly synchronize portable terminal plugins',
	spec: `A terminal connecting to a different Hal home compares explicitly portable plugin files with that host after connection without delaying the usable prompt. Same-home clients do not compare a home against itself. Recompare changed portable files while connected; reconnect uses the existing transport and does not duplicate pending comparisons or file operations. Use the history and version rules of task gev. Exchange inventories first and transfer differing contents and relevant history on demand, not the whole archive at every connection. Retain prior portable identity for deletion comparisons. Do not execute an incoming differing plugin merely to preview it.

Neither side wins automatically, including when one is newer. Expose equal, client-ahead, server-ahead, divergent or unrelated versions, dates and missing files to /plugin-sync. The later recorded UTC timestamp suggests a choice without hiding divergence; ties have no timestamp-based winner. User confirmation names source and destination. Selecting an absent source deletes the destination; an empty source creates an empty file. Preserve both versions and deletion history before replacement. Check both current version identities/hashes against the confirmed comparison immediately before applying; stale decisions refresh the comparison instead of overwriting unseen edits. Concurrent clients cannot apply stale decisions to shared host files. Failed writes, disconnects and lost acknowledgments leave recoverable history, report complete errors and do not turn reconnect into an unconfirmed overwrite. Reuse existing operation acknowledgment/deduplication and atomic writes. Validate filenames, hashes and message shapes so file operations stay within the intended top-level plugin paths. Existing tokens suffice; no new authorization model.

plugins/.syncignore contains excluded top-level plugin filenames, one per line, allowing blank lines and comments; no general glob engine is needed. Respect exclusions on either endpoint before comparing or transferring contents. Keep separate writes the entry only on the connecting client, leaves both plugin files untouched, and removes the discrepancy. Its scope is that client home across servers; a server-side entry excludes the filename across its clients. Ignored files still load, hot-reload and record history locally; removing an exclusion resumes comparison. /plugin-sync can explain which end excluded a file. Config sync, automatic transfer, automatic merges, provider-assisted merging, dependency-package transfer and per-server ignore exceptions are deferred. Terminal feature only: no web-specific implementation. Test two-home comparison, portable expiry, missing/empty/deleted files, either-side ignores, ancestry, stale answers, concurrent changes and reconnect deduplication.`,
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['tr', 'gev'],
	foldInto: ['an']
}
